::ALG2_U04_L06_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve log<sub>2</sub>(x)=3. {
= x = 8#Correct. Rewrite as x = 2<sup>3</sup>.
~ x = 6#This multiplies 2 and 3.
~ x = 5#This adds 2 and 3.
~ x = 1#This subtracts the output from the base.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve log<sub>5</sub>(x)=2. {
~ x = 10#This multiplies base and output.
= x = 25#Correct. Rewrite as x = 5<sup>2</sup>.
~ x = 7#This adds base and output.
~ x = 3#This subtracts incorrectly.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve log<sub>3</sub>(x + 4)=2. {
~ x = 9#This forgets to subtract 4.
~ x = 13#This adds 4 instead of subtracting.
= x = 5#Correct. Rewrite as x + 4 = 9, so x = 5.
~ x = 6#This adds base and output.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve log<sub>4</sub>(x - 2)=3. {
~ x = 64#This forgets to add 2.
~ x = 12#This multiplies base and output.
~ x = 5#This adds base and output only.
= x = 66#Correct. Rewrite as x - 2 = 64, so x = 66.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 3<sup>x</sup> = 27. {
= x = 3#Correct. Since 27 = 3<sup>3</sup>, x = 3.
~ x = 9#This divides 27 by 3.
~ x = 24#This subtracts 3 from 27.
~ x = 30#This adds 3 and 27.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 4<sup>x</sup> = 64. {
~ x = 16#This divides 64 by 4.
= x = 3#Correct. Since 64 = 4<sup>3</sup>, x = 3.
~ x = 60#This subtracts 4 from 64.
~ x = 256#This multiplies 64 by 4.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 10<sup>x</sup> = 1000. {
~ x = 100#This divides 1000 by 10.
~ x = 990#This subtracts 10 from 1000.
= x = 3#Correct. Since 1000 = 10<sup>3</sup>, x = 3.
~ x = 4#10 to the fourth is 10000.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 2<sup>x</sup> = 7 using logarithms. {
~ x = log(2)/log(7)#This reverses the ratio.
~ x = 7/2#This treats the equation as linear.
~ x = 14#This multiplies the base and output.
= x = log(7)/log(2)#Correct. Change of base isolates the exponent.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 3<sup>x</sup> = 20 using logarithms. {
= x = log(20)/log(3)#Correct. Change of base gives the exponent.
~ x = log(3)/log(20)#This reverses the ratio.
~ x = 20/3#This treats the equation as linear.
~ x = 60#This multiplies the base and output.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve log<sub>2</sub>(x - 6)=4. {
~ x = 16#This forgets to add 6.
= x = 22#Correct. Rewrite as x - 6 = 16, so x = 22.
~ x = 10#This adds the log output and 6 only.
~ x = -22#This has the wrong sign.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve log<sub>6</sub>(x)=1. {
~ x = 1#6 to the first power is 6, not 1.
~ x = 0#6 to the zero power is 1, but the log output is 1.
= x = 6#Correct. Any base to the first power equals itself.
~ x = 7#This adds base and output.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve log<sub>7</sub>(x)=0. {
~ x = 0#No valid logarithm input gives base to a power equal to 0.
~ x = 7#7 to the first power is 7.
~ x = -1#Logarithm inputs must be positive.
= x = 1#Correct. Any positive base to the zero power equals 1.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which candidate is invalid for log<sub>3</sub>(x - 5)? {
= x = 4#Correct. The input x - 5 would be negative.
~ x = 6#The input is 1, which is positive.
~ x = 10#The input is 5, which is positive.
~ x = 20#The input is 15, which is positive.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve log<sub>2</sub>(x + 1)=0. {
~ x = 1#This forgets to subtract 1.
= x = 0#Correct. Rewrite as x + 1 = 1, so x = 0.
~ x = -1#This makes the log input zero, which is invalid.
~ x = 2#This uses the base as the solution.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve log<sub>5</sub>(2x)=2. {
~ x = 25#This forgets to divide by 2.
~ x = 10#This multiplies the base and output.
= x = 12.5#Correct. Rewrite as 2x = 25, so x = 12.5.
~ x = 7#This adds base and output.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 9<sup>x</sup> = 81. {
~ x = 9#This is the base, not the exponent.
~ x = 72#This subtracts incorrectly.
~ x = 729#This multiplies instead of solving for the exponent.
= x = 2#Correct. Since 81 = 9<sup>2</sup>, x = 2.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve log<sub>4</sub>(x)=1/2. {
= x = 2#Correct. Rewrite as x = 4<sup>1/2</sup>, which equals 2.
~ x = 8#This multiplies 4 by 2.
~ x = 16#This squares 4 instead of taking the square root.
~ x = 1/8#This uses a negative exponent idea incorrectly.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 6<sup>x</sup> = 13 using logarithms. {
~ x = log(6)/log(13)#This reverses the ratio.
= x = log(13)/log(6)#Correct. Use change of base to isolate x.
~ x = 13/6#This treats the equation as linear.
~ x = 78#This multiplies the base and output.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve log<sub>8</sub>(x - 3)=1. {
~ x = 8#This forgets to add 3.
~ x = 4#This adds output and 3 only.
= x = 11#Correct. Rewrite as x - 3 = 8, so x = 11.
~ x = -11#This has the wrong sign.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve log<sub>10</sub>(x)=2. {
~ x = 20#This multiplies 10 and 2.
~ x = 12#This adds 10 and 2.
~ x = 1000#This uses exponent 3.
= x = 100#Correct. Rewrite as x = 10<sup>2</sup>.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>What should be checked after solving a logarithmic equation? {
= All logarithm inputs are positive.#Correct. Logarithms require positive inputs in the original equation.
~ The output is always positive.#Logarithm outputs may be negative, zero, or positive.
~ The base is the solution.#The base is not usually the solution.
~ Every answer is extraneous.#Some candidates are valid after checking.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve log<sub>3</sub>(2x - 1)=2. {
~ x = 9#This does not solve the linear expression after rewriting.
= x = 5#Correct. Rewrite as 2x - 1 = 9, so 2x = 10 and x = 5.
~ x = 4#This forgets the +1 before dividing by 2.
~ x = 10#This forgets to divide by 2.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 5<sup>x</sup> = 125. {
~ x = 25#This divides 125 by 5.
~ x = 120#This subtracts 5.
= x = 3#Correct. Since 125 = 5<sup>3</sup>, x = 3.
~ x = 625#This multiplies by 5.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve log<sub>2</sub>(x/3)=4. {
~ x = 16#This forgets to multiply by 3.
~ x = 12#This multiplies the exponent by 3 instead of using 2 to the fourth.
~ x = 7#This adds 3 and 4.
= x = 48#Correct. Rewrite as x/3 = 16, so x = 48.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 7<sup>x</sup> = 50 using logarithms. {
= x = log(50)/log(7)#Correct. Change of base gives x as a ratio of logarithms.
~ x = log(7)/log(50)#This reverses the ratio.
~ x = 50/7#This treats the equation as linear.
~ x = 350#This multiplies the base and output.
}

